      "started_at": "2026-11-06T15:12:28.100729+00:00",
      "result": {
        "reply_text": "You can complain to the National Consumer Helpline by visiting their web portal, using their app, emailing, messaging on WhatsApp, or calling their toll-free numbers. You can also file a complaint before a District Commission.\n\nKeep your invoice, cancellation email, and bank statement ready as proof.\n\nYour next steps are to register your grievance with the National Consumer Helpline, and if needed, you can file a complaint with the District Commission.\n\nWhat I understood: CloudBasket cancelled your order for shoes and did not refund your money, and support tickets were closed without resolution.\n\nEvery e-commerce entity must appoint a grievance officer who must acknowledge a consumer complaint within forty-eight hours and redress it within one month from receipt. Also, e-commerce entities must effect refunds for requests made according to the rules within a reasonable period of time.\n\nThe District Commission can entertain complaints where the value of the goods or services paid as consideration does not exceed up to fifty lakh rupees or one crore rupees.\n\nA complaint to the District Commission must be filed within two years from the date on which the cause of action has arisen, unless the commission permits a delay for sufficient cause.\n\nSource: Department of Consumer Affairs - National Consumer Helpline (NCH), H NCH\nSource: National Consumer Helpline Grievance Redressal Procedure, procedure 1\nSource: National Consumer Helpline Guidelines, helpline 1915\nSource: Consumer Protection Act, 2019, section 35\nSource: Consumer Protection (E-Commerce) Rules, 2020, rule 4\nSource: Consumer Protection Act, 2019, section 34\nSource: Consumer Protection (Jurisdiction of the District Commission, the State Commission and the National Commission) Rules, 2021, rule 3\nSource: Consumer Protection Act, 2019, section 69\n\nThis is legal information, not legal advice.",
